{
  "$comment": "Shared canonical vocabulary for the hard-filter fields. Both parsers (ai/app/schemas.py), the seed check (api seed) and the Go API's write path resolve free text to these ids, so a JD's required_certifications and a profile's certifications compare exactly in SQL. Matching is by normalised key: lower-case, '&' -> 'and', parentheticals dropped, the words certified/certification/certificate/credential/license/licensed/licence dropped, then every non-alphanumeric character removed. 'QuickBooks Online', 'QBO' and 'Quick-Books' therefore all resolve to 'quickbooks'. Ids are snake_case and never change once shipped; add aliases instead. Free text that does not resolve is kept in the schema's other_* fields, never dropped. Accounting qualifications carry more than a name: 'group' is the equivalence group (see qualification_groups), 'issuing_body' / 'body_aliases' and 'jurisdictions' say who awards it and where, and 'variant_of' marks a body-specific qualification whose letters are ambiguous on their own ('CPA' is US, Canadian or Australian; 'CA' and 'ACA' belong to several institutes). The ambiguous ids (cpa, ca, aca) are what bare letters resolve to; the parsers move them to a variant when the issuing body or the candidate's location settles it (ai/app/qualifications.py). A requirement is met by its own id, by any variant of it and, when the role accepts equivalents, by every body-specific member of the same group; an ambiguous id held by a candidate never counts as a group member.",
